# shellcheck configuration for this repo.
#
# CI runs `shellcheck -x` over every *.sh via tests/test-shell-syntax.sh and
# requires zero output — informational and style findings included. So this file
# is the actual code-style enforcement here, not a suggestion.
#
# `source`d paths are resolved, matching the -x the test already passes.
external-sources=true

# Optional checks are off by default in shellcheck. The five below were each
# measured against the whole tree first and found to produce zero findings, so
# turning them on enforces nothing new today and catches a regression tomorrow.
# That is the only kind of rule worth adding to a suite that must stay at zero.
enable=quote-safe-variables
enable=check-unassigned-uppercase
enable=add-default-case
enable=avoid-nullary-conditions
enable=deprecate-which

# Deliberately NOT enabled, with the finding count each would add today:
#
#   require-variable-braces    (23)  ${var} everywhere, including $1 and $#
#   require-double-brackets    (11)  ci/write-badges.sh uses [ ] throughout
#   check-extra-masked-returns (51)  flags the local x=$(...) idiom the tests
#                                    are built on
#
# None of those is a defect. Enabling one means rewriting working, reviewed
# code to satisfy a style preference, which is a separate decision from
# "shellcheck must be clean" — make it on purpose, in its own PR, if at all.
